data source_authority_module_note: String = "Wave 2 Gate-A flip lane: v2.compiler.source_authority is the cross-tree ingest and round-trip authority (parse→normalize→resolve→serialize laws over DagSourceReadWitness / SourceRootIngest). Curated seed-linked scaffold defers full tree behavioral oracle until emitter Rc/Optional coherence (#6775) greens rustc on the closure."

type DagSourceAst {
  tree: ParseTree
}

type NormalizedDagSourceAst {
  tree: NormalizedTree
}

type DagSemanticIr {
  tree: ResolvedTree
}

type DagSourceReadWitness {
  source: Medium<String>
  artifact: Artifact

  compilation_unit: Symbol

  source_root: SourceRootRef
}

type SourceRootIngest = FreeMonoid<DagSourceReadWitness>

data source_root_coverage_note: String = "Capless closure transport (ingest-manifest-source-ref-carrier design stage 4): the manifest emitter no longer inlines Lossless source text. Every read is carried as a SourceRef row in host_source_root_closure_refs (path + source root + content hash). host_source_root_ingest stays Empty; consumers read through refs with hash verification at consumption time.

manifest_inline_list_max (64) is the historical inline-text capacity boundary. Past it the INLINE carrier is refused via SourceRootManifestElided { read_count, cap } — typed, located, counted — while the closure-ref carrier still carries the full population (produced_row_count == ingest_read_count). Overflow must never render as zero rows and an empty ingest (DESIGN empty-observation narrow). At or below the cap, coverage is SourceRootCoverageComplete."

data manifest_inline_list_max: Int = 64

type SourceRootCoverage
  = SourceRootCoverageComplete
  | SourceRootManifestElided { read_count: Int, cap: Int }
  | SourceRootRowsMissing { read_count: Int, produced_row_count: Int }
  | SourceRootManifestAbsent

type DiscoveredSourceRefsDigestUnavailableCause
  = DiscoveredSourceRefsDigestNoRefList { reason: String }

type DiscoveredSourceRefsDigest
  = DiscoveredSourceRefsDigestFromList { digest: Fnv1a64Structural }
  | DiscoveredSourceRefsDigestUnavailable {
      cause: DiscoveredSourceRefsDigestUnavailableCause
    }

// CLOSED (operator ruling 2026-08-04, merge requirement): population identity lives in
// FrontierProbeDiscoveredClosure — refs and population_digest are sealed together by
// frontier_probe_discovered_closure_seal and cannot be authored as independent halves. Host
// manifest consumption binds receipt digest to the sealed closure in
// frontier_probe_discovered_closure_subject_from_host_manifest before probe execution.

type FrontierProbeDiscoveredClosure {
  refs: List<SourceRef>,
  population_digest: DiscoveredSourceRefsDigest,
  entry: SourceRef
}

// Closed probe-side closure carrier (operator ruling 2026-08-04): refs, population_digest, and
// entry are one subject. population_digest is always derived from refs at seal time — never a
// separate caller input.

fn frontier_probe_discovered_closure_seal(
  refs: List<SourceRef>,
  entry: SourceRef
) -> FrontierProbeDiscoveredClosure {
  FrontierProbeDiscoveredClosure {
    refs: refs,
    population_digest: discovered_source_refs_digest_for_refs(refs: refs),
    entry: entry
  }
}

type SourceRootProvenanceCoverageReceipt {
  ingest_read_count: Int
  produced_row_count: Int
  discovered_source_refs_digest: DiscoveredSourceRefsDigest
  coverage: SourceRootCoverage
}

data source_root_coverage_arms_note: String = "THREE arms, not two, because the two producers fail for different reasons and their remedies differ. SourceRootManifestElided is the HOST emitter's refusal: a declared cap rejected the whole row list, and the fix is a bigger cap or a carrier that does not inline source text. SourceRootRowsMissing is the IN-GRAPH derivation's refusal: produced rows do not match reads with NO cap involved, which means rows were lost somewhere and the fix is finding that bug. Collapsing them would have forced the in-graph derivation to name a cap it does not have — fabricating a number to satisfy a shape (DESIGN section 5). Distinct causes, distinct arms, each carrying only numbers its own producer actually knows.

A fourth arm, SourceRootManifestAbsent, is the COMMITTED STUB's state: no host manifest has been supplied at this source root yet. It is not Complete (that would be a vacuous truth over zero rows, exactly the shape a witness can ride green while proving nothing), it is not RowsMissing (nothing was read, so 0 against 0 is not a shortfall), and it is not Elided (no cap rejected anything). Its remedy is different too: run the emitting gate, or order the overlay root after the stub. The stub previously said coverage_complete: false, which was fail-closed but mute about which of these four it meant."

data source_root_coverage_is_complete_note: String = "A DELIBERATE one-question projection, not a dissolution back to the Bool this coproduct replaced (review 39781 raised exactly this, and the distinction is worth stating precisely).

What it answers: 'is coverage complete?' — nothing else. It discards WHICH deficit occurred, and that discard is correct at its call sites, which are all ASSERTIONS: a witness asserting complete coverage needs a boolean, and inlining the same four-arm match at each of them would be four copies of one decision (DESIGN section 2). The predicate is the DRY form of a fact the coproduct still owns.

What it is NOT: the old coverage_complete field. That was the receipt's ONLY representation of coverage, so a consumer had no way to learn the cause — the information did not exist anywhere. Now the cause is carried on the receipt, and any consumer that needs it matches SourceRootCoverage directly and gets read_count, cap, or produced_row_count. Nothing is lost; one caller-chosen question is answered.

It carries no Disposition because it is not a scaffold: there is no future state in which this becomes wrong, so there is no honest dissolution trigger to declare, and inventing one would be a scaffold marker that can never fire (DESIGN section 6 — every scaffold lands with a named trigger, which equally means a non-scaffold must not fake one). The rule it must keep: this predicate may never become the only way to read coverage. If a consumer ever needs the reason and reaches for a Bool instead, that consumer is wrong, not this projection."

fn source_root_coverage_is_complete(coverage: SourceRootCoverage) -> Bool {
  match coverage {
    SourceRootCoverageComplete => true
    SourceRootManifestElided { read_count: _, cap: _ } => false
    SourceRootRowsMissing { read_count: _, produced_row_count: _ } => false
    SourceRootManifestAbsent => false
  }
}

// TWO CARRIERS, TWO COMPLETENESS QUESTIONS, NEITHER ANSWERING FOR THE OTHER.
// source_root_coverage_is_complete asks whether the INLINE carrier is complete. This asks whether
// the CLOSURE-REF carrier is, and the two differ on exactly one variant.
//
// SourceRootManifestElided is the host emitter reporting that a declared cap rejected the inline
// row list (source_root_coverage_arms_note states it in those words). Past that cap the ref
// population stays complete -- produced_row_count still equals ingest_read_count -- which the
// manifest carrier note also states. So for a consumer that reads through refs and never touches
// the inline text, Elided is a fact about a carrier it does not consume, and refusing on it is a
// wrong-subject check rather than a strict one. It refused every closure over the cap regardless of
// contents: the compiler's own closure is 107 modules against a cap of 64, so the first executed
// seven-boundary product receipt refused at boundary 2 for a reason that had nothing to do with its
// subject.
//
// WHAT THIS PREDICATE STILL REFUSES, and why the arm is narrowed rather than deleted.
// SourceRootRowsMissing is the in-graph derivation losing rows with no cap involved -- a real
// deficit for either carrier. SourceRootManifestAbsent is the committed stub, where nothing has
// been supplied at all; admitting it would be a vacuous truth over zero rows. Deleting the coverage
// arm outright would LOOK safe, because source_root_closure_ref_transport_complete independently
// catches RowsMissing (its produced_row_count == ingest_read_count clause is exactly that variant's
// negation) -- but that makes the guarantee incidental to arm ORDER, and a later reorder would walk
// a real deficit through. A replacement preserves every required refusal or it has erased a
// correctness distinction (operator ruling, smart-ram-730, 2026-08-21).
//
// WHY IT LIVES HERE and not as an inline match in the driver: the coverage coproduct is this
// module's, so a second unnamed answer to its completeness question authored at a call site would
// be exactly the nicknaming this repository consolidates later at cost.

fn source_root_ref_transport_coverage_admits(coverage: SourceRootCoverage) -> Bool {
  match coverage {
    SourceRootCoverageComplete => true
    SourceRootManifestElided { read_count: _, cap: _ } => true
    SourceRootRowsMissing { read_count: _, produced_row_count: _ } => false
    SourceRootManifestAbsent => false
  }
}

fn source_root_closure_ref_transport_complete(
  receipt: SourceRootProvenanceCoverageReceipt,
  refs: List<SourceRef>
) -> Bool {
  (receipt.ingest_read_count > 0)
    && (receipt.produced_row_count == receipt.ingest_read_count)
    && (length(xs: refs) == receipt.ingest_read_count)
}

// Positive ingest_read_count requires produced_row_count and closure-ref population to match —
// never zero rows with a positive read count (pre-capless empty-observation narrow).
fn source_root_overflow_never_empty_population_holds(
  receipt: SourceRootProvenanceCoverageReceipt,
  refs: List<SourceRef>
) -> Bool {
  if receipt.ingest_read_count > 0 {
    source_root_closure_ref_transport_complete(receipt: receipt, refs: refs)
  } else {
    true
  }
}

type SourceIrArtifactProvenance {
  node: Node
  artifact: Artifact
  span_index: SpanIndex
}

data source_ref_authority_note: String = "Stage 0 of docs/plans/ingest-manifest-source-ref-carrier-design.md §6: the typed reference to a STORAGE REALIZATION. Declared here, with the path⇄module binding authority, because both the ingest-manifest rows and the later Phase-3 host-effect boundary must consume ONE spelling — established before it has two consumers rather than reconciled after them (§3).

What it names is deliberately NOT a module. A manifest row is a host-boundary artifact about files the host read, and only host boundaries project paths, so a path-shaped ref is correct exactly there; module identity, where one exists, is the DERIVED fact ModuleStorageBinding already owns and is JOINED to a ref, never fused into it. A ModuleRef | FileRef coproduct was rejected for that reason: it would fuse the two facts this lane exists to keep apart (parent ruling 2026-07-19).

This is a TYPE DECLARATION ONLY. The effect-boundary enforcement that makes bare-string file dependencies unwritable is Phase 3 and does not land here."

data source_ref_no_fidelity_note: String = "SourceRef carries NO DecodeFidelity field, and that is a modeling decision rather than an omission. Fidelity is a property of a DECODE — how faithfully bytes became a value — and it already has a single authority in Medium<R> { carried, fidelity } (dag/extdeps/communication/medium.dag). A ref decodes nothing; it names a location. A fidelity ON the ref would therefore assert how faithfully something decodes BEFORE any read established it (§5 fabricated plausible output), and would stand a second fidelity authority beside Medium's (§3).

The requirement that a row declare its fidelity is real and is ALREADY SATISFIED: DagSourceReadWitness holds source: Medium<String>, which carries it. The constraint this places on the later carrier migration — the stage-4 read-through must return Medium<String>, never a bare String, or the Lossless boundary silently goes undeclared — is recorded as named constraint C1 in the design note, because that failure produces nothing red at the diff."

data source_ref_seed_homonym_note: String = "NAME COLLISION, deliberate and marked. src/v1/01_tokenize.dag:53 declares a DIFFERENT type also called SourceRef — a tokenizer input handle { file, text, source_chars } that CARRIES the source bytes. The two are inverted on the exact axis this type turns on: that one contains the source, this one exists precisely so rows do not.

The seed's use is itself the nickname violation (a ref that carries everything references nothing), so the correct name goes to the correct use rather than the correct use being renamed around a misnomer — renaming here would invert which use is authoritative and fork this spelling from the design docs' at the moment of declaration (§3; parent ruling 2026-07-19). No hard collision exists: the two resolve by subtree, and the fork-prevention wall keys on identical variant payloads, which two differently-shaped records do not trigger.

dissolve-on: the v1 tokenizer being next touched or deleted, at which point the seed's type is renamed to SourceBuffer — the honest name for a thing holding bytes. The v1_delete milestone discharges this naturally. The note is homed HERE rather than in src/v1/01_tokenize.dag so that an inert marker costs zero touches on a load-bearing v1 pipeline stage, and anyone about to collide with the name finds it by grepping the name itself."

type SourceStorageIdentity {
  path: String
  source_root: SourceRootRef
}

// Storage realization identity for a manifest SourceRef row: path plus source_root only. Content hash is a separate observation layered on SourceRef. Closure population uniqueness is enforced at storage identity; two rows sharing storage but disagreeing on content_hash is a typed storage-hash conflict, not two valid refs.
type SourceRef {
  path: String
  source_root: SourceRootRef
  content_hash: ContentHash
}

fn source_storage_identity_from_ref(ref: SourceRef) -> SourceStorageIdentity {
  SourceStorageIdentity {
    path: ref.path,
    source_root: ref.source_root
  }
}

fn source_storage_identity_eq(left: SourceStorageIdentity, right: SourceStorageIdentity) -> Bool {
  (left.path == right.path) && (left.source_root == right.source_root)
}

fn source_root_ref_identity_digest(root: SourceRootRef) -> Fnv1a64Structural {
  match root {
    V2Tree => content_hash_atom(value: "V2Tree")
    DagTree => content_hash_atom(value: "DagTree")
  }
}

fn source_storage_identity_digest(storage: SourceStorageIdentity) -> Fnv1a64Structural {
  content_hash_combine_structural(
    left: content_hash_atom(value: storage.path as NonEmptyStr),
    right: source_root_ref_identity_digest(root: storage.source_root)
  )
}

type SourceRefContentHashStructural
  = SourceRefContentHashStructuralOk { digest: Fnv1a64Structural }
  | SourceRefContentHashStructuralNonFnv1a64 { family: HashFamily }

fn source_ref_content_hash_structural(hash: ContentHash) -> SourceRefContentHashStructural {
  match hash {
    Fnv1a64(structural) => SourceRefContentHashStructuralOk { digest: structural }
    Sha256Hash(_) => SourceRefContentHashStructuralNonFnv1a64 { family: content_hash_family(hash: hash) }
    Sha1Hash(_) => SourceRefContentHashStructuralNonFnv1a64 { family: content_hash_family(hash: hash) }
    Sha512Hash(_) => SourceRefContentHashStructuralNonFnv1a64 { family: content_hash_family(hash: hash) }
  }
}

fn source_ref_identity_digest_from_structural(
  ref: SourceRef,
  structural: Fnv1a64Structural
) -> Fnv1a64Structural {
  content_hash_combine_structural(
    left: source_storage_identity_digest(
      storage: source_storage_identity_from_ref(ref: ref)
    ),
    right: structural
  )
}

type SourceRefListDigestBuild
  = SourceRefListDigestOk { digest: Fnv1a64Structural }
  | SourceRefListDigestNonStructuralRefused { path: String }

data source_ref_list_digest_seed: String = "source-ref-closure-list"

fn source_ref_list_digest_build(refs: List<SourceRef>) -> SourceRefListDigestBuild {
  match fold_list(
    xs: refs,
    empty: SourceRefListDigestOk {
      digest: content_hash_atom(value: source_ref_list_digest_seed)
    },
    cons: fn(acc, ref) {
      match acc {
        SourceRefListDigestNonStructuralRefused { path: _ } => acc
        SourceRefListDigestOk { digest: prior } =>
          match source_ref_content_hash_structural(hash: ref.content_hash) {
            SourceRefContentHashStructuralNonFnv1a64 { family: _ } =>
              SourceRefListDigestNonStructuralRefused { path: ref.path }
            SourceRefContentHashStructuralOk { digest: structural } =>
              SourceRefListDigestOk {
                digest: content_hash_combine_structural(
                  left: prior,
                  right: source_ref_identity_digest_from_structural(
                    ref: ref,
                    structural: structural
                  )
                )
              }
          }
      }
    }
  ) {
    SourceRefListDigestNonStructuralRefused { path: path } =>
      SourceRefListDigestNonStructuralRefused { path: path }
    SourceRefListDigestOk { digest: d } =>
      SourceRefListDigestOk { digest: d }
  }
}

fn source_ref_list_digest(refs: List<SourceRef>) -> Fnv1a64Structural {
  match source_ref_list_digest_build(refs: refs) {
    SourceRefListDigestOk { digest: d } => d
    SourceRefListDigestNonStructuralRefused { path: _ } =>
      content_hash_atom(value: "source-ref-list-digest-non-structural-refused")
  }
}

fn discovered_source_refs_digest_for_refs(refs: List<SourceRef>) -> DiscoveredSourceRefsDigest {
  match source_ref_list_digest_build(refs: refs) {
    SourceRefListDigestOk { digest: d } =>
      DiscoveredSourceRefsDigestFromList { digest: d }
    SourceRefListDigestNonStructuralRefused { path: _ } =>
      DiscoveredSourceRefsDigestUnavailable {
        cause: DiscoveredSourceRefsDigestNoRefList {
          reason: "non-fnv1a64-content-hash-in-closure-ref"
        }
      }
  }
}

fn source_storage_count_in_list(
  refs: List<SourceRef>,
  target: SourceStorageIdentity
) -> Int {
  fold_list(
    xs: refs,
    empty: 0,
    cons: fn(count, ref) {
      if source_storage_identity_eq(
        left: source_storage_identity_from_ref(ref: ref),
        right: target
      ) {
        count + 1
      } else {
        count
      }
    }
  )
}

fn source_storage_list_has_duplicate_storage(refs: List<SourceRef>) -> Bool {
  fold_list(
    xs: refs,
    empty: false,
    cons: fn(found, ref) {
      if found {
        true
      } else {
        source_storage_count_in_list(
          refs: refs,
          target: source_storage_identity_from_ref(ref: ref)
        ) > 1
      }
    }
  )
}

fn source_storage_hash_conflict_in_list(refs: List<SourceRef>) -> Bool {
  fold_list(
    xs: refs,
    empty: false,
    cons: fn(found, ref) {
      if found {
        true
      } else {
        let storage = source_storage_identity_from_ref(ref: ref)
        fold_list(
          xs: refs,
          empty: false,
          cons: fn(inner_found, other) {
            if inner_found {
              true
            } else {
              let other_storage = source_storage_identity_from_ref(ref: other)
              if source_storage_identity_eq(left: storage, right: other_storage)
                && !source_ref_eq(left: ref, right: other) {
                true
              } else {
                false
              }
            }
          }
        )
      }
    }
  )
}

// Selection-grain placeholder hash for declared SourceRef rows
// (docs/plans/declared-source-ref-selection-design.md §7): content-hash-as-selection-key belongs to
// the resolve-cost lane, not this task. Rows carry the field for shape compatibility with the
// eventual pinned authority; selection today keys on storage path resolved through
// ModuleStorageIndex only.

fn source_ref_path(ref: SourceRef) -> String {
  ref.path
}

fn source_ref_for_storage_path(path: String, source_root: SourceRootRef) -> SourceRef {
  SourceRef {
    path: path,
    source_root: source_root,
    content_hash: content_hash_of_value(value: "selection-unpinned" as NonEmptyStr)
  }
}

fn source_ref_eq(left: SourceRef, right: SourceRef) -> Bool {
  let paths_match = left.path == right.path
  let roots_match = left.source_root == right.source_root
  let hashes_match =
    match compare_content_hash(left: left.content_hash, right: right.content_hash) {
      ContentHashEqual => true
      ContentHashDifferent => false
      ContentHashCrossFamilyIncomparable => false
    }
  paths_match && roots_match && hashes_match
}

fn source_ref_count_in_list(refs: List<SourceRef>, target: SourceRef) -> Int {
  fold_list(
    xs: refs,
    empty: 0,
    cons: fn(count, ref) {
      if source_ref_eq(left: ref, right: target) {
        count + 1
      } else {
        count
      }
    }
  )
}

fn source_ref_list_has_duplicate(refs: List<SourceRef>) -> Bool {
  fold_list(
    xs: refs,
    empty: false,
    cons: fn(found, ref) {
      if found {
        true
      } else {
        source_ref_count_in_list(refs: refs, target: ref) > 1
      }
    }
  )
}

data module_storage_binding_authority_note: String = "Phase 1 of docs/plans/module-identity-storage-binding-design.md §2: a module is a node (containment subtree); a file is ONE storage realization of module content (§2 Realization). The path⇄module binding is therefore DERIVED at parse — the compiler parsed the files and knows which modules came out — never authored. This is the single home for that fact. It once shared the job with 27 hand-authored FrontierQualifiedModuleBinding rows in the self-host frontier module, kept in step by a standing parity witness; those rows and that witness are deleted with the frontier roster, so the duality this sentence used to describe as an interim is closed and the host-side build_module_path_index is now the only other projection. Task 4 repointed the host producer through v1_compiler_parse (src/v1/stage0/src/module_path_index); DeclarationScanned deleted. Scope is the LIVE 1:1 binding the tree enforces today; many-to-many (fragment identity/order, several-modules-one-file) is a NAMED DEFERRAL until a consumer prices it (§6), never a silent assumption. What lands now is the provenance coproduct, so zero-file produced modules are representable honestly rather than pretending they came from parse."

type ModuleStorageProvenance
  = ParsedFromSource { artifact: Artifact, span_index: SpanIndex }
  | ProducedByBehavior { producer: Symbol }

type ModuleStorageBinding {
  module: QualifiedName
  provenance: ModuleStorageProvenance
  source_root: SourceRootRef
}

type ModuleStorageIndex = FreeMonoid<ModuleStorageBinding>

type ModuleStorageFoldState
  = ModuleStorageFoldOk {
      bindings: ModuleStorageIndex
    }
  | ModuleStorageFoldFailed {
      diagnostics: NonEmptyDiagnostics
    }

type SourceAstEqual {
  original: DagSourceAst
  reparsed: DagSourceAst
}

type SemanticIrEqual {
  original: DagSemanticIr
  reparsed: DagSemanticIr
}

type NormalizedSourceAstEqual {
  original: NormalizedDagSourceAst
  reparsed: NormalizedDagSourceAst
}

type DeterministicDagSourceSerializerLaw {
  canonical_section: String
  declared_fidelity: DecodeFidelity
}

type NormalizedDagSourceParsePrintLaw {
  normalized_source_law: NormalizedSourceAstEqual
}

type CanonicalDagSourceEmissionLaw {
  canonical_source: Medium<String>
  serializer_law: DeterministicDagSourceSerializerLaw
  parse_print_law: NormalizedDagSourceParsePrintLaw
}

type SourceAuthorityRoundTripLaw {
  canonical_source: Medium<String>
  source_law: SourceAstEqual
  semantic_law: SemanticIrEqual
  canonical_source_law: CanonicalDagSourceEmissionLaw
}

fn source_authority_diagnostic(reason: Symbol) -> Diagnostic {
  Diagnostic {
    reason: reason,
    at: invariant_port_locus(port: ^source_authority_port, invariant: reason),
    correction: Unavailable { reason: ExternalContractUnknown }
  }
}

fn parse_dag_source_ast_with_model(
  source: Medium<String>,
  compilation_unit: Symbol,
  lm: LanguageModel
) -> Outcome<DagSourceAst> {
  bind_outcome(
    o: tokenize(text: source.carried, file: compilation_unit, rules: lm.lex),
    f: fn(token_stream) {
      bind_outcome(
        o: parse(tokens: token_stream, grammar: lm.grammar),
        f: fn(parse_tree) {
          outcome_accepted(value: DagSourceAst { tree: parse_tree })
        }
      )
    }
  )
}

fn parse_dag_source_ast(source: Medium<String>) -> Outcome<DagSourceAst> {
  parse_dag_source_ast_with_model(
    source: source,
    compilation_unit: ^source_authority_port,
    lm: dag_language_model()
  )
}

fn normalize_dag_source_ast(ast: DagSourceAst) -> Outcome<NormalizedDagSourceAst> {
  bind_outcome(
    o: normalize(parse_tree: ast.tree),
    f: fn(normalized) {
      outcome_accepted(value: NormalizedDagSourceAst { tree: normalized })
    }
  )
}

fn semantic_ir_from_ast_with_model(ast: DagSourceAst, lm: LanguageModel) -> Outcome<DagSemanticIr> {
  bind_outcome(
    o: normalize(parse_tree: ast.tree),
    f: fn(normalized) {
      bind_outcome(
        o: resolve(tree: normalized, lm: lm),
        f: fn(resolved) {
          outcome_accepted(value: DagSemanticIr { tree: resolved })
        }
      )
    }
  )
}

fn semantic_ir_from_source_with_model(
  source: Medium<String>,
  compilation_unit: Symbol,
  lm: LanguageModel
) -> Outcome<DagSemanticIr> {
  bind_outcome(
    o: parse_dag_source_ast_with_model(
      source: source,
      compilation_unit: compilation_unit,
      lm: lm
    ),
    f: fn(ast) {
      semantic_ir_from_ast_with_model(ast: ast, lm: lm)
    }
  )
}

fn semantic_ir_from_source(source: Medium<String>) -> Outcome<DagSemanticIr> {
  semantic_ir_from_source_with_model(
    source: source,
    compilation_unit: ^source_authority_port,
    lm: dag_language_model()
  )
}

fn source_ir_artifact_provenance_from_model(
  source_read: DagSourceReadWitness,
  lm: LanguageModel
) -> Outcome<SourceIrArtifactProvenance> {
  match source_read.artifact.kind {
    SourceFile =>
      bind_outcome(
        o: tokenize(
          text: source_read.source.carried,
          file: source_read.compilation_unit,
          rules: lm.lex
        ),
        f: fn(token_stream) {
          bind_outcome(
            o: parse_module(tokens: token_stream, grammar: lm.grammar),
            f: fn(artifact) {
              outcome_accepted(
                value: SourceIrArtifactProvenance {
                  node: artifact.tree,
                  artifact: source_read.artifact,
                  span_index: artifact.span_index
                }
              )
            }
          )
        }
      )
    GeneratedSource =>
      outcome_rejected(d: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_read_artifact_not_source_file))
    TestClaimArtifact =>
      outcome_rejected(d: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_read_artifact_not_source_file))
    ProjectionArtifact =>
      outcome_rejected(d: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_read_artifact_not_source_file))
    Stage0Candidate =>
      outcome_rejected(d: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_read_artifact_not_source_file))
    CorePackage =>
      outcome_rejected(d: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_read_artifact_not_source_file))
    WitnessBundle =>
      outcome_rejected(d: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_read_artifact_not_source_file))
  }
}

fn source_ir_artifact_provenance(
  source_read: DagSourceReadWitness
) -> Outcome<SourceIrArtifactProvenance> {
  source_ir_artifact_provenance_from_model(
    source_read: source_read,
    lm: dag_language_model()
  )
}

fn module_storage_binding_from_provenance(
  prov: SourceIrArtifactProvenance,
  source_root: SourceRootRef
) -> Outcome<ModuleStorageBinding> {
  match qualified_name_from_module_node(root: prov.node) {
    Accepted { value: qn, diagnostics: _ } =>
      outcome_accepted(
        value: ModuleStorageBinding {
          module: qn,
          provenance: ParsedFromSource {
            artifact: prov.artifact,
            span_index: prov.span_index
          },
          source_root: source_root
        }
      )
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } =>
      Rejected { diagnostics: d }
  }
}

// The name-derivation refusal FORWARDS the derivation's own diagnostics unchanged rather than
// substituting a port-level source_authority_diagnostic. Substituting would satisfy 'typed' while
// destroying 'located' (§5 requires both): qualified_name_from_module_node reports at the offending
// node, whereas source_authority_port locates only the stage. Dropping that is the same loss of
// signal the silent-drop arm caused, one tier milder — the caller learns THAT a module name failed
// but not WHERE. This also keeps the authority path identical to the sibling arm in
// v2.compiler.program_assembly, so one derivation failure does not produce two different
// diagnostics depending on which entry point ran.

// prepare_grammar is hoisted ABOVE the fold, exactly as v2.compiler.program_assembly does
// (program_assembly_prepare_once_note): the grammar is well-formed-checked, validated, and
// FIRST/nullable-analyzed ONCE per ingest, then parse_module_prepared reuses the PreparedGrammar
// for every read. Deriving the binding over a K-module ingest therefore validates the grammar once,
// not K times — the recompute-per-module cost this dissolves (§6 bare-minimum cost: a proven
// cost-shape defect is always fixed, and 'K is small here' is not a time-stable fact — K becomes
// the whole tree once the projections replace the hand tables). Empty ingest is guarded so it never
// pays the one-time prepare for zero reads, and an invalid grammar fails the whole derivation
// before any read is parsed, carrying the validation diagnostic — never a per-read silent skip.

fn module_storage_binding_from_parse_artifact(
  artifact: ParseArtifact,
  source_read: DagSourceReadWitness
) -> Outcome<ModuleStorageBinding> {
  module_storage_binding_from_provenance(
    prov: SourceIrArtifactProvenance {
      node: artifact.tree,
      artifact: source_read.artifact,
      span_index: artifact.span_index
    },
    source_root: source_read.source_root
  )
}

fn module_storage_binding_from_read_prepared(
  source_read: DagSourceReadWitness,
  lm: LanguageModel,
  prepared: PreparedGrammar
) -> Outcome<ModuleStorageBinding> {
  match source_read.artifact.kind {
    SourceFile =>
      bind_outcome(
        o: tokenize(
          text: source_read.source.carried,
          file: source_read.compilation_unit,
          rules: lm.lex
        ),
        f: fn(token_stream) {
          bind_outcome(
            o: parse_module_prepared(
              tokens: token_stream,
              prepared: prepared
            ),
            f: fn(artifact) {
              module_storage_binding_from_parse_artifact(
                artifact: artifact,
                source_read: source_read
              )
            }
          )
        }
      )
    GeneratedSource =>
      outcome_rejected(d: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_read_artifact_not_source_file))
    TestClaimArtifact =>
      outcome_rejected(d: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_read_artifact_not_source_file))
    ProjectionArtifact =>
      outcome_rejected(d: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_read_artifact_not_source_file))
    Stage0Candidate =>
      outcome_rejected(d: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_read_artifact_not_source_file))
    CorePackage =>
      outcome_rejected(d: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_read_artifact_not_source_file))
    WitnessBundle =>
      outcome_rejected(d: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_read_artifact_not_source_file))
  }
}

fn module_storage_binding_from_read_with_model(
  source_read: DagSourceReadWitness,
  lm: LanguageModel
) -> Outcome<ModuleStorageBinding> {
  match prepare_grammar(grammar: lm.grammar) {
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } => Rejected { diagnostics: d }
    Accepted { value: prepared, diagnostics: _ } =>
      module_storage_binding_from_read_prepared(
        source_read: source_read,
        lm: lm,
        prepared: prepared
      )
  }
}

// §5 — a failure arm REFUSES, never widens. A read whose module name cannot be derived is a located refusal, NOT a dropped binding: the pre-Phase-1 derivation in v2.compiler.program_assembly kept the root and left the index unchanged on Rejected, so a module that failed name extraction became silently invisible to every downstream lookup (an absorbing fallback in drop form — the deficit's frequency zeroed by construction). Duplicate module within the index is likewise a typed refusal here: it is the modeled form of the host panic at src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs module_path_collision_panic_message, so the live 1:1 binding is enforced by the model rather than incidentally by a Rust panic.
fn module_storage_binding_module(binding: ModuleStorageBinding) -> QualifiedName {
  binding.module
}

fn module_storage_binding_module_dotted(binding: ModuleStorageBinding) -> String {
  qualified_name_to_dotted_string(qn: binding.module)
}

// Module identity compares STRUCTURALLY on QualifiedName (= FreeMonoid<Symbol>), never through a
// rendered dotted string. Routing identity through qualified_name_to_dotted_string would allocate
// two strings per comparison — n per insert, so n^2 string builds over an ingest — to answer a
// question the symbol list already answers, and it would make a rendering function load-bearing for
// identity (§6 bare-minimum cost; §3 the dotted form is a projection FOR DISPLAY, not the identity
// authority). The hand-rolled qualified_name_eq was already dissolved into structural == when
// QualifiedName became a FreeMonoid alias; this reuses that, it does not re-mint it.

fn module_storage_index_contains_module(
  index: ModuleStorageIndex,
  module: QualifiedName
) -> Bool {
  fold_list(
    xs: index,
    empty: false,
    cons: fn(found, binding) {
      if found {
        true
      } else {
        module_storage_binding_module(binding: binding) == module
      }
    }
  )
}

fn module_storage_index_insert(
  index: ModuleStorageIndex,
  binding: ModuleStorageBinding
) -> Outcome<ModuleStorageIndex> {
  if module_storage_index_contains_module(index: index, module: binding.module) {
    outcome_rejected(
      d: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_module_storage_binding_duplicate)
    )
  } else {
    outcome_accepted(value: list_snoc_item(xs: index, item: binding))
  }
}

fn module_storage_fold_step(
  state: ModuleStorageFoldState,
  read: DagSourceReadWitness,
  lm: LanguageModel,
  prepared: PreparedGrammar
) -> ModuleStorageFoldState {
  match state {
    ModuleStorageFoldFailed { diagnostics: d } =>
      ModuleStorageFoldFailed { diagnostics: d }
    ModuleStorageFoldOk { bindings: acc } =>
      match module_storage_binding_from_read_prepared(
        source_read: read,
        lm: lm,
        prepared: prepared
      ) {
        Rejected { diagnostics: d } =>
          ModuleStorageFoldFailed { diagnostics: d }
        Accepted { value: binding, diagnostics: _ } =>
          match module_storage_index_insert(index: acc, binding: binding) {
            Rejected { diagnostics: d } =>
              ModuleStorageFoldFailed { diagnostics: d }
            Accepted { value: next, diagnostics: _ } =>
              ModuleStorageFoldOk { bindings: next }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn module_storage_bindings_from_ingest_prepared(
  ingest: SourceRootIngest,
  lm: LanguageModel,
  prepared: PreparedGrammar
) -> Outcome<ModuleStorageIndex> {
  match fold_list(
    xs: ingest,
    empty: ModuleStorageFoldOk { bindings: Empty },
    cons: fn(state, read) {
      module_storage_fold_step(
        state: state,
        read: read,
        lm: lm,
        prepared: prepared
      )
    }
  ) {
    ModuleStorageFoldOk { bindings: b } => outcome_accepted(value: b)
    ModuleStorageFoldFailed { diagnostics: d } => Rejected { diagnostics: d }
  }
}

fn module_storage_bindings_from_ingest_with_model(
  ingest: SourceRootIngest,
  lm: LanguageModel
) -> Outcome<ModuleStorageIndex> {
  match ingest {
    Empty => outcome_accepted(value: Empty)
    _ =>
      match prepare_grammar(grammar: lm.grammar) {
        Rejected { diagnostics: d } => Rejected { diagnostics: d }
        Accepted { value: prepared, diagnostics: _ } =>
          module_storage_bindings_from_ingest_prepared(
            ingest: ingest,
            lm: lm,
            prepared: prepared
          )
      }
  }
}

fn module_storage_bindings_from_ingest(
  ingest: SourceRootIngest
) -> Outcome<ModuleStorageIndex> {
  module_storage_bindings_from_ingest_with_model(
    ingest: ingest,
    lm: dag_language_model()
  )
}

// The zero-file case, representable by construction (§2): the body producer already creates modules
// with no file at all, which is the standing proof that identity cannot be the path.
// ProducedByBehavior carries the producing behavior instead of an artifact, so such a module enters
// the binding table honestly rather than being excluded (today
// source_ir_artifact_provenance_from_model rejects every non-SourceFile artifact, so produced
// modules cannot enter provenance at all).

fn module_storage_produced_binding(
  module: QualifiedName,
  producer: Symbol,
  source_root: SourceRootRef
) -> ModuleStorageBinding {
  ModuleStorageBinding {
    module: module,
    provenance: ProducedByBehavior { producer: producer },
    source_root: source_root
  }
}

fn module_storage_parsed_binding(
  module: QualifiedName,
  artifact: Artifact,
  span_index: SpanIndex,
  source_root: SourceRootRef
) -> ModuleStorageBinding {
  ModuleStorageBinding {
    module: module,
    provenance: ParsedFromSource {
      artifact: artifact,
      span_index: span_index
    },
    source_root: source_root
  }
}

fn module_storage_binding_file_path(binding: ModuleStorageBinding) -> Optional<String> {
  match binding.provenance {
    ParsedFromSource { artifact: a, span_index: _ } =>
      optional_present(value: a.file_path)
    ProducedByBehavior { producer: _ } =>
      optional_absent()
  }
}

// THE BINDING TABLE'S ONLY PRODUCER IS THE IN-GRAPH DERIVATION, module_storage_bindings_from_ingest: it
// parses each module of an ingest and records module, path and provenance -- deliberately no source
// text, which is why this carrier differs from SourceRootIngest. The host manifest emitter that once
// supplied the same rows from a whole-repository walk is deleted by the source layout cutover
// (gunbc.plans.source_layout_authority): a module's file is decided by its name and the layout rule
// (v2.cli.repository_layout), and a driver acquires only the modules it requests, so no walk exists to
// enumerate paths and no second supply remains to be reconciled with the derivation.

// Both requested directions are queries against ONE table (design §2). file → graph region: given a
// path, the module parsed from it. module → file(s): given a module reference, its current storage
// — needed ONLY at host boundaries; user code and tool .dag reference modules and never see paths.
// A produced module answers Absent for storage, which is a real answer, not a miss.

fn module_storage_module_for_path(
  index: ModuleStorageIndex,
  file_path: String
) -> Optional<QualifiedName> {
  fold_list(
    xs: index,
    empty: optional_absent(),
    cons: fn(found, binding) {
      match found {
        Present { value: v } => optional_present(value: v)
        Absent =>
          match module_storage_binding_file_path(binding: binding) {
            Present { value: p } =>
              if p == file_path {
                optional_present(value: module_storage_binding_module(binding: binding))
              } else {
                optional_absent()
              }
            Absent => optional_absent()
          }
      }
    }
  )
}

fn module_storage_path_for_module(
  index: ModuleStorageIndex,
  module: QualifiedName
) -> Optional<String> {
  fold_list(
    xs: index,
    empty: optional_absent(),
    cons: fn(found, binding) {
      match found {
        Present { value: v } => optional_present(value: v)
        Absent =>
          if module_storage_binding_module(binding: binding) == module {
            module_storage_binding_file_path(binding: binding)
          } else {
            optional_absent()
          }
      }
    }
  )
}

fn source_ir_node_artifact_provenance_from_model(
  source_read: DagSourceReadWitness,
  lm: LanguageModel
) -> Outcome<NodeArtifactProvenance> {
  match source_ir_artifact_provenance_from_model(source_read: source_read, lm: lm) {
    Accepted { value: prov, diagnostics: _ } =>
      outcome_accepted(
        value: NodeArtifactProvenance {
          node: prov.node,
          artifact: prov.artifact
        }
      )
    Rejected { diagnostics: r } => Rejected { diagnostics: r }
  }
}

fn source_ir_node_artifact_provenance(
  source_read: DagSourceReadWitness
) -> Outcome<NodeArtifactProvenance> {
  source_ir_node_artifact_provenance_from_model(
    source_read: source_read,
    lm: dag_language_model()
  )
}

type NodeArtifactProvenanceFoldState
  = NodeArtifactProvenanceFoldOk {
      provenance: FreeMonoid<NodeArtifactProvenance>
    }
  | NodeArtifactProvenanceFoldFailed {
      diagnostics: NonEmptyDiagnostics
    }

fn node_artifact_provenance_fold_step(
  state: NodeArtifactProvenanceFoldState,
  read: DagSourceReadWitness
) -> NodeArtifactProvenanceFoldState {
  match state {
    NodeArtifactProvenanceFoldFailed { diagnostics: d } =>
      NodeArtifactProvenanceFoldFailed { diagnostics: d }
    NodeArtifactProvenanceFoldOk { provenance: acc } =>
      match source_ir_node_artifact_provenance(source_read: read) {
        Accepted { value: row, diagnostics: _ } =>
          NodeArtifactProvenanceFoldOk {
            provenance: list_snoc_item(xs: acc, item: row)
          }
        Rejected { diagnostics: d } =>
          NodeArtifactProvenanceFoldFailed { diagnostics: d }
      }
  }
}

fn node_artifact_provenance_from_source_root(
  ingest: SourceRootIngest
) -> Outcome<FreeMonoid<NodeArtifactProvenance>> {
  match fold_list(
    xs: ingest,
    empty: NodeArtifactProvenanceFoldOk { provenance: Empty },
    cons: fn(state, read) {
      node_artifact_provenance_fold_step(state: state, read: read)
    }
  ) {
    NodeArtifactProvenanceFoldOk { provenance: p } =>
      outcome_accepted(value: p)
    NodeArtifactProvenanceFoldFailed { diagnostics: d } =>
      Rejected { diagnostics: d }
  }
}

fn source_root_provenance_coverage_receipt(
  ingest: SourceRootIngest,
  produced: FreeMonoid<NodeArtifactProvenance>
) -> SourceRootProvenanceCoverageReceipt {
  SourceRootProvenanceCoverageReceipt {
    ingest_read_count: length(xs: ingest),
    produced_row_count: length(xs: produced),
    discovered_source_refs_digest: DiscoveredSourceRefsDigestUnavailable {
      cause: DiscoveredSourceRefsDigestNoRefList {
        reason: "ingest-provenance-path-no-ref-list"
      }
    },
    coverage: if length(xs: ingest) == length(xs: produced) {
      SourceRootCoverageComplete
    } else {
      SourceRootRowsMissing {
        read_count: length(xs: ingest),
        produced_row_count: length(xs: produced)
      }
    }
  }
}

fn source_root_set_from_ingest(ingest: SourceRootIngest) -> SourceRootSet {
  fold_list(
    xs: ingest,
    empty: Empty,
    cons: fn(acc, read) {
      source_root_set_insert(roots: acc, r: read.source_root)
    }
  )
}

fn source_authority_node_equal(left: Node, right: Node) -> Bool {
  node_equal_ignoring_provenance(a: left, b: right)
}

fn source_ast_equal_witness(
  original: DagSourceAst,
  reparsed: DagSourceAst
) -> Witness<SourceAstEqual> {
  if source_authority_node_equal(left: original.tree, right: reparsed.tree) {
    Holds { value: SourceAstEqual { original: original, reparsed: reparsed } }
  } else {
    Violates {
      diagnostic: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_ast_mismatch)
    }
  }
}

// THE PROJECTION OUT OF THE SEALED CARRIER, WHICH THIS CALL SITE OWED SINCE BL-1.
// `NormalizedTree` was an alias for `Node` until BL-1 sealed it into a sole-constructor record so
// that wrapper-retention evidence could not reach resolve as a bare `Node`. Sealing it made
// `NormalizedDagSourceAst.tree` a carrier rather than a node, and `source_authority_node_equal`
// asks for nodes. The neighbouring `source_ast_equal_witness` reads correctly only because
// `DagSourceAst.tree` is a `ParseTree`, still an alias -- so the two sites look identical and are
// not. `.root` is `v2.compiler.normalized_tree`'s own projection out of the carrier, the same one
// `normalized_tree_roots_to_nodes` uses.
fn normalized_source_ast_equal_witness(
  original: NormalizedDagSourceAst,
  reparsed: NormalizedDagSourceAst
) -> Witness<NormalizedSourceAstEqual> {
  if source_authority_node_equal(left: original.tree.root, right: reparsed.tree.root) {
    Holds { value: NormalizedSourceAstEqual { original: original, reparsed: reparsed } }
  } else {
    Violates {
      diagnostic: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_normalized_ast_mismatch)
    }
  }
}

fn semantic_ir_equal_witness(
  original: DagSemanticIr,
  reparsed: DagSemanticIr
) -> Witness<SemanticIrEqual> {
  if source_authority_node_equal(left: original.tree.root, right: reparsed.tree.root) {
    Holds { value: SemanticIrEqual { original: original, reparsed: reparsed } }
  } else {
    Violates {
      diagnostic: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_ir_mismatch)
    }
  }
}

fn deterministic_dag_source_serializer_witness(
  first: Medium<String>,
  second: Medium<String>
) -> Witness<DeterministicDagSourceSerializerLaw> {
  if medium_section_equal(left: first, right: second) {
    Holds {
      value: DeterministicDagSourceSerializerLaw {
        canonical_section: first.carried,
        declared_fidelity: first.fidelity
      }
    }
  } else {
    Violates {
      diagnostic: source_authority_diagnostic(reason: ^source_authority_serializer_nondeterministic)
    }
  }
}

fn canonical_dag_source_emission_law_from_parts(
  original_ast: DagSourceAst,
  canonical_source_first: Medium<String>,
  canonical_source_second: Medium<String>,
  reparsed_ast: DagSourceAst
) -> Outcome<Witness<CanonicalDagSourceEmissionLaw>> {
  bind_outcome(
    o: normalize_dag_source_ast(ast: original_ast),
    f: fn(original_normalized_ast) {
      bind_outcome(
        o: normalize_dag_source_ast(ast: reparsed_ast),
        f: fn(reparsed_normalized_ast) {
          let serializer_law = deterministic_dag_source_serializer_witness(
            first: canonical_source_first,
            second: canonical_source_second
          )
          let parse_print_law = normalized_source_ast_equal_witness(
            original: original_normalized_ast,
            reparsed: reparsed_normalized_ast
          )
          match serializer_law {
            Violates { diagnostic: d } =>
              Rejected { diagnostics: diagnostics_singleton(d: d) }
            Holds { value: serializer_law_value } =>
              match parse_print_law {
                Violates { diagnostic: d } =>
                  Rejected { diagnostics: diagnostics_singleton(d: d) }
                Holds { value: parse_print_law_value } =>
                  outcome_accepted(
                    value: Holds {
                      value: CanonicalDagSourceEmissionLaw {
                        canonical_source: canonical_source_first,
                        serializer_law: serializer_law_value,
                        parse_print_law: NormalizedDagSourceParsePrintLaw {
                          normalized_source_law: parse_print_law_value
                        }
                      }
                    }
                  )
              }
          }
        }
      )
    }
  )
}

fn source_authority_law_from_parts(
  original_ast: DagSourceAst,
  canonical_source: Medium<String>,
  reparsed_ast: DagSourceAst,
  original_ir: DagSemanticIr,
  reparsed_ir: DagSemanticIr,
  canonical_source_law: CanonicalDagSourceEmissionLaw
) -> Outcome<Witness<SourceAuthorityRoundTripLaw>> {
  let source_law = source_ast_equal_witness(original: original_ast, reparsed: reparsed_ast)
  let semantic_law = semantic_ir_equal_witness(original: original_ir, reparsed: reparsed_ir)
  match source_law {
    Violates { diagnostic: d } =>
      Rejected { diagnostics: diagnostics_singleton(d: d) }
    Holds { value: source_law_value } =>
      match semantic_law {
        Violates { diagnostic: d } =>
          Rejected { diagnostics: diagnostics_singleton(d: d) }
        Holds { value: semantic_law_value } =>
          outcome_accepted(
            value: Holds {
              value: SourceAuthorityRoundTripLaw {
                canonical_source: canonical_source,
                source_law: source_law_value,
                semantic_law: semantic_law_value,
                canonical_source_law: canonical_source_law
              }
            }
          )
      }
  }
}

fn source_authority_round_trip_with_model(
  source: Medium<String>,
  canonical_source_target: TargetModel,
  lm: LanguageModel
) -> Outcome<Witness<SourceAuthorityRoundTripLaw>> {
  bind_outcome(
    o: parse_dag_source_ast_with_model(
      source: source,
      compilation_unit: ^source_authority_port,
      lm: lm
    ),
    f: fn(original_ast) {
      bind_outcome(
        o: semantic_ir_from_ast_with_model(ast: original_ast, lm: lm),
        f: fn(original_ir) {
          bind_outcome(
            o: target_serialize_source_from_model(
              target: canonical_source_target,
              emitted: original_ir.tree.root
            ),
            f: fn(canonical_source) {
              bind_outcome(
                o: target_serialize_source_from_model(
                  target: canonical_source_target,
                  emitted: original_ir.tree.root
                ),
                f: fn(canonical_source_again) {
                  bind_outcome(
                    o: parse_dag_source_ast_with_model(
                      source: canonical_source,
                      compilation_unit: ^source_authority_port,
                      lm: lm
                    ),
                    f: fn(reparsed_ast) {
                      bind_outcome(
                        o: semantic_ir_from_ast_with_model(ast: reparsed_ast, lm: lm),
                        f: fn(reparsed_ir) {
                          bind_outcome(
                            o: canonical_dag_source_emission_law_from_parts(
                              original_ast: original_ast,
                              canonical_source_first: canonical_source,
                              canonical_source_second: canonical_source_again,
                              reparsed_ast: reparsed_ast
                            ),
                            f: fn(canonical_source_law_witness) {
                              match canonical_source_law_witness {
                                Violates { diagnostic: d } =>
                                  Rejected { diagnostics: diagnostics_singleton(d: d) }
                                Holds { value: canonical_source_law } =>
                                  source_authority_law_from_parts(
                                    original_ast: original_ast,
                                    canonical_source: canonical_source,
                                    reparsed_ast: reparsed_ast,
                                    original_ir: original_ir,
                                    reparsed_ir: reparsed_ir,
                                    canonical_source_law: canonical_source_law
                                  )
                              }
                            }
                          )
                        }
                      )
                    }
                  )
                }
              )
            }
          )
        }
      )
    }
  )
}

fn canonical_dag_source_parse_print_law(
  source: Medium<String>,
  canonical_source_target: TargetModel
) -> Outcome<Witness<CanonicalDagSourceEmissionLaw>> {
  bind_outcome(
    o: parse_dag_source_ast(source: source),
    f: fn(original_ast) {
      bind_outcome(
        o: semantic_ir_from_ast_with_model(
          ast: original_ast,
          lm: dag_language_model()
        ),
        f: fn(original_ir) {
          bind_outcome(
            o: target_serialize_source_from_model(
              target: canonical_source_target,
              emitted: original_ir.tree.root
            ),
            f: fn(canonical_source) {
              bind_outcome(
                o: target_serialize_source_from_model(
                  target: canonical_source_target,
                  emitted: original_ir.tree.root
                ),
                f: fn(canonical_source_again) {
                  bind_outcome(
                    o: parse_dag_source_ast(source: canonical_source),
                    f: fn(reparsed_ast) {
                      canonical_dag_source_emission_law_from_parts(
                        original_ast: original_ast,
                        canonical_source_first: canonical_source,
                        canonical_source_second: canonical_source_again,
                        reparsed_ast: reparsed_ast
                      )
                    }
                  )
                }
              )
            }
          )
        }
      )
    }
  )
}

fn source_authority_round_trip(
  source: Medium<String>,
  canonical_source_target: TargetModel
) -> Outcome<Witness<SourceAuthorityRoundTripLaw>> {
  source_authority_round_trip_with_model(
    source: source,
    canonical_source_target: canonical_source_target,
    lm: dag_language_model()
  )
}

// ---- the storage rule (gunbc.plans.source_layout_authority, R5) --------------------------------
//
// THE ONE FACT THAT MAPS A MODULE NAME TO ITS FILE. Module `a.b.c` lives at `<root(a)>/b/c.dag`:
// the namespace root decides which tree holds it and whether the top segment is part of the
// relative path (the v2 tree elides `v2`, every other tree keeps its namespace as the first
// directory). Paths follow names, never the reverse. The rule is expressed over a LOGICAL tree
// (SourceRootRef) and a relative path and never over a host path, so a storage binding supplies the
// host directory and nothing here knows it. The repository's own roots are POLICY and arrive as a
// SourceStorageLayout value at the entry boundary; this module imports no repository fact.
// `namespace_rows` are the namespace rows (gunbc.source_layout_namespaces) in their order; the one membership
// read over them (v2.std.cross_tree.import_model namespace_declared) is asked by the storage lookup per
// candidate and by the population enumeration per listed module.
type StorageNamespaceRoot {
  namespace: String
  tree: SourceRootRef
  elides_namespace: Bool
}

type SourceStorageLayout {
  namespace_roots: List<StorageNamespaceRoot>
  default_tree: SourceRootRef
  namespace_rows: List<String>
  derived_rosters: List<StorageDerivedRoster>
}

fn source_storage_layout(
  namespace_roots: List<StorageNamespaceRoot>,
  default_tree: SourceRootRef,
  namespace_rows: List<String>,
  derived_rosters: List<StorageDerivedRoster>
) -> SourceStorageLayout {
  SourceStorageLayout {
    namespace_roots: namespace_roots,
    default_tree: default_tree,
    namespace_rows: namespace_rows,
    derived_rosters: derived_rosters
  }
}

// A LEDGER NAMESPACE WHOSE ROSTER MODULE HAS NO FILE (gunbc.source_layout LayoutDerivedRoster is the
// repository's policy; the rule is below). The rows live one per module directly under
// `row_namespace`, and `<row_namespace>.roster` is the list of them, derived by acquisition from the
// sibling modules that declare a `row_type` row under their own stem.
type StorageDerivedRoster {
  row_namespace: String
  row_type: String
}

type ModuleFileRef {
  tree: SourceRootRef
  relative_path: String
}

type ModuleFileDerivation
  = ModuleFileDerived { file: ModuleFileRef }
  | ModuleNameUnderivable { name: String, cause: String }

fn storage_namespace_root_for(layout: SourceStorageLayout, namespace: String) -> Optional<StorageNamespaceRoot> {
  match list_head(xs: filter(xs: layout.namespace_roots, predicate: fn(r) { r.namespace == namespace })) {
    Present { value: root } => optional_present(value: root)
    Absent => optional_absent()
  }
}

fn module_file_relative_path(segments: List<String>) -> String {
  concat(join(segments, "/"), ".dag")
}

// A SEGMENT IS A MODULE-SYNTAX IDENTIFIER, decided by the alphabet the tokenizer lexes an identifier
// with (v2.compiler.tokenize char_in_class), never by a second list here. The path rule joins segments
// with `/`, so a segment admitted with a separator in it would derive the file of a different name
// (`a/b` and `a.b` would both be stored at a/b.dag) and a name would stop being the key of its file.
// Refusing every non-identifier segment before any path is derived makes the name-to-path map
// injective over the names it accepts, and a derived path stays beneath its tree.
fn module_name_segment_is_identifier(segment: String) -> Bool {
  let scalars = unicode_scalar_unfold(s: segment)
  match string_head(s: scalars) {
    CharAbsent => false
    CharFound { value: first } =>
      char_in_class(c: first, class: IdentStartChar)
        && fold_list(xs: scalars, empty: true, cons: fn(ok, c) { ok && char_in_class(c: c, class: IdentContinueChar) })
  }
}

fn module_name_has_non_identifier_segment(segments: List<String>) -> Bool {
  fold_list(xs: segments, empty: false, cons: fn(found, segment) { found || !module_name_segment_is_identifier(segment: segment) })
}

fn segment_or_empty(segments: List<String>, at: Int) -> String {
  match list_head(xs: skip(xs: segments, n: at)) {
    Present { value: segment } => segment
    Absent => ""
  }
}

fn segment_count(segments: List<String>) -> Int {
  fold_list(xs: segments, empty: 0, cons: fn(n, segment) { n + 1 })
}

// The file a module name is stored at. A name is a nonempty dotted sequence of identifier segments;
// anything else is refused with its cause, before any path is derived. The namespace a layout elides is a directory that does
// not exist, so a name made of only that namespace has no file.
fn module_file(layout: SourceStorageLayout, name: String) -> ModuleFileDerivation {
  let segments = split(s: name, delimiter: ".")
  if name == "" || module_name_has_non_identifier_segment(segments: segments) {
    ModuleNameUnderivable { name: name, cause: "a module name is a nonempty dotted sequence of identifier segments (letters, digits and underscore, not starting with a digit)" }
  } else {
    match storage_namespace_root_for(layout: layout, namespace: segment_or_empty(segments: segments, at: 0)) {
      Present { value: root } =>
        if root.elides_namespace {
          if segment_count(segments: segments) == 1 {
            ModuleNameUnderivable { name: name, cause: concat("the namespace ", concat(root.namespace, " is a directory of its tree and has no file of its own")) }
          } else {
            ModuleFileDerived { file: ModuleFileRef { tree: root.tree, relative_path: module_file_relative_path(segments: skip(xs: segments, n: 1)) } }
          }
        } else {
          ModuleFileDerived { file: ModuleFileRef { tree: root.tree, relative_path: module_file_relative_path(segments: segments) } }
        }
      Absent =>
        ModuleFileDerived { file: ModuleFileRef { tree: layout.default_tree, relative_path: module_file_relative_path(segments: segments) } }
    }
  }
}

fn source_root_ref_label(tree: SourceRootRef) -> String {
  match tree {
    V2Tree => "v2"
    DagTree => "dag"
  }
}

// The module name a file's position implies: the inverse of module_file, derived from the same
// rows. A path that is not a `.dag` file has no name. The caller compares module_file of the
// derived name with the file to decide whether the file is at its rule position; a position the
// rule cannot reach (a case-folded segment, a stem with a dot) therefore compares unequal rather
// than being silently normalised.
type ModuleNameForFile
  = ModuleNameDerived { name: String }
  | ModuleFileNotNamed { file: ModuleFileRef, cause: String }

fn module_name_for_file(layout: SourceStorageLayout, file: ModuleFileRef) -> ModuleNameForFile {
  if !file.relative_path.ends_with(suffix: ".dag") {
    ModuleFileNotNamed { file: file, cause: "a module file ends in .dag" }
  } else {
    let stem = substring(s: file.relative_path, start: 0, end: string_length(s: file.relative_path) - 4)
    let dotted = replace(s: stem, from: "/", to: ".")
    let elided = filter(xs: layout.namespace_roots, predicate: fn(r) {
      r.elides_namespace && source_root_ref_label(tree: r.tree) == source_root_ref_label(tree: file.tree)
    })
    match list_head(xs: elided) {
      Present { value: root } => ModuleNameDerived { name: concat(root.namespace, concat(".", dotted)) }
      Absent => ModuleNameDerived { name: dotted }
    }
  }
}

// ---- derived roster modules -------------------------------------------------------------
//
// ONE RULE FOR EVERY ONE-ROW-PER-FILE LEDGER. The rows are the authority for the ledger's membership;
// a hand-appended list of them is a second authoring of the same fact, and a list written to disk
// is a second authority beside the rows (DESIGN section 3). So the roster is a module with no file,
// and its text is a function of the sibling modules alone:
//
//   module    <row_namespace>.roster
//   members   every module directly under <row_namespace> that declares `data <stem>: <row_type> =`
//             under its own file stem; a helper module beside the rows declares none and is not one
//   order     the sorted stems, which are the declaration names
//   value     `data <last segment of row_namespace>_roster: List<row_type> = [ <members> ]`
//
// Names and order are DERIVED from the namespace and the listing, never declared per ledger: the
// policy row carries only what cannot be derived (which namespaces are ledgers, which type selects a
// row). The v1 seed realizes the same contract in v1_compiler.cli_run.derived_row_roster
// (DERIVED_ROW_ROSTERS) and is the regression oracle for this rule, not its specification.
//
// TWO REFUSALS, BOTH CORRECTIONS OF A SILENT SEED ARM. A sibling that declares a `row_type` row under
// a name other than its stem is refused here, located (DerivedRosterRowMisplaced): the seed drops such
// a row from the list and leaves its discovery to an off-path parse join, so natively it would simply
// vanish. And the reading of a declaration line tolerates the spacing the formatter may write around
// `:` and `=`, where the seed matched one exact spelling of the line.
//
// THE SELECTION READS DECLARATION LINES, NOT THE PARSE, and that is named rather than hidden: it is
// discovery over the source text, exact for the formatter's one-line `data` head, and a head split
// across lines is not seen. Its next rung is declaration introspection that BINDS a discovered data
// declaration as a typed value (gunbc.guarantee_stall.roster_re_enumerates_its_own_rows_stall), at
// which point no roster module is needed at all and this section deletes.
fn derived_roster_stem() -> String {
  "roster"
}

fn derived_roster_module(roster: StorageDerivedRoster) -> String {
  concat(roster.row_namespace, ".", derived_roster_stem())
}

fn derived_roster_namespace_leaf(roster: StorageDerivedRoster) -> String {
  match list_head(xs: list_reverse(xs: split(s: roster.row_namespace, delimiter: "."))) {
    Present { value: leaf } => leaf
    Absent => roster.row_namespace
  }
}

fn derived_roster_declaration(roster: StorageDerivedRoster) -> String {
  concat(derived_roster_namespace_leaf(roster: roster), "_", derived_roster_stem())
}

// The ledger whose roster `module` is, if it is one.
fn derived_roster_named(layout: SourceStorageLayout, module: String) -> Optional<StorageDerivedRoster> {
  list_find_first(xs: layout.derived_rosters, predicate: fn(r) { derived_roster_module(roster: r) == module })
}

fn derived_roster_row_module(roster: StorageDerivedRoster, stem: String) -> String {
  concat(roster.row_namespace, ".", stem)
}

// The ledger `module` is a sibling of, if it is one: a module directly under a roster's row namespace,
// other than the roster itself. Its bytes are an input of that roster's derivation.
fn derived_roster_sibling_of(layout: SourceStorageLayout, module: String) -> Optional<StorageDerivedRoster> {
  list_find_first(xs: layout.derived_rosters, predicate: fn(r) {
    let prefix = concat(r.row_namespace, ".")
    module.starts_with(prefix: prefix)
      && module != derived_roster_module(roster: r)
      && length(xs: split(s: substring(s: module, start: string_length(s: prefix), end: string_length(s: module)), delimiter: ".")) == 1
  })
}

// One sibling of a roster: a module directly under the row namespace, by its file stem, with the bytes
// acquisition read for it.
type DerivedRosterSibling {
  stem: String
  content: String
}

type DerivedRosterSelection
  = DerivedRosterSelected { members: List<String> }
  | DerivedRosterRowMisplaced { stem: String, declared: String }

// The declaration a line heads, when the line is a `data` head declaring a value of `row_type`.
fn derived_roster_line_row(line: String, row_type: String) -> Optional<String> {
  if !line.starts_with(prefix: "data ") {
    optional_absent()
  } else {
    let rest = substring(s: line, start: 5, end: string_length(s: line))
    let at_colon = split(s: rest, delimiter: ":")
    match list_head(xs: at_colon) {
      Absent => optional_absent()
      Present { value: name } =>
        match list_head(xs: split(s: join(skip(xs: at_colon, n: 1), ":"), delimiter: "=")) {
          Absent => optional_absent()
          Present { value: declared_type } =>
            if trim(s: declared_type) == row_type && string_length(s: trim(s: name)) > 0 {
              optional_present(value: trim(s: name))
            } else {
              optional_absent()
            }
        }
    }
  }
}

// Every `row_type` row a sibling declares, by declaration name, in source order.
fn derived_roster_sibling_rows(content: String, row_type: String) -> List<String> {
  fold_list(xs: split(s: content, delimiter: "\n"), empty: [], cons: fn(acc, line) {
    match derived_roster_line_row(line: line, row_type: row_type) {
      Present { value: name } => list_snoc_item(xs: acc, item: name)
      Absent => acc
    }
  })
}

type DerivedRosterSelectAcc {
  members: List<String>
  misplaced: Optional<DerivedRosterSelection>
}

fn derived_roster_select_sibling(acc: DerivedRosterSelectAcc, sibling: DerivedRosterSibling, row_type: String) -> DerivedRosterSelectAcc {
  match acc.misplaced {
    Present { value: _ } => acc
    Absent =>
      fold_list(xs: derived_roster_sibling_rows(content: sibling.content, row_type: row_type), empty: acc, cons: fn(inner, declared) {
        match inner.misplaced {
          Present { value: _ } => inner
          Absent =>
            if declared == sibling.stem {
              DerivedRosterSelectAcc { members: list_snoc_item(xs: inner.members, item: declared), misplaced: inner.misplaced }
            } else {
              DerivedRosterSelectAcc {
                members: inner.members,
                misplaced: optional_present(value: DerivedRosterRowMisplaced { stem: sibling.stem, declared: declared })
              }
            }
        }
      })
  }
}

// THE MEMBERSHIP: the siblings that declare a row under their own stem, sorted by stem. The siblings
// arrive in any order; the roster's order is the rule's, never the listing's.
fn derived_roster_select(roster: StorageDerivedRoster, siblings: List<DerivedRosterSibling>) -> DerivedRosterSelection {
  let selected = fold_list(
    xs: siblings,
    empty: DerivedRosterSelectAcc { members: [], misplaced: optional_absent() },
    cons: fn(acc, sibling) { derived_roster_select_sibling(acc: acc, sibling: sibling, row_type: roster.row_type) }
  )
  match selected.misplaced {
    Present { value: refusal } => refusal
    Absent => DerivedRosterSelected { members: sort_by(selected.members, m => m) }
  }
}

// THE ROSTER MODULE'S TEXT over its sorted members. The bytes are the seed's render_roster's, so a
// consumer that reads the derived module natively and one that reads it through the seed compile one
// module, and the positive control (v2.test.claim.derived_roster_seed_agreement) compares the two.
fn derived_roster_text(roster: StorageDerivedRoster, members: List<String>) -> String {
  let header = join([
    concat("module ", derived_roster_module(roster: roster)),
    "",
    concat("// DERIVED from sibling files declaring a ", roster.row_type, " row under their own stem. Do not"),
    "// hand-edit. Membership is the directory filtered by declared type; order is the sorted",
    "// filename stem, which is the declaration name. An append is a new file in this",
    "// directory, never an edit here.",
    "",
    "import std.types { List }",
    concat("import ", roster.row_namespace, " { ", roster.row_type, " }"),
    ""
  ], "\n")
  let imports = join(list_map(xs: members, f: fn(m) { concat("import ", derived_roster_row_module(roster: roster, stem: m), " { ", m, " }\n") }), "")
  let entries = join(list_map(xs: members, f: fn(m) { concat("  ", m, ",\n") }), "")
  concat(
    header,
    imports,
    "\n",
    concat("data ", derived_roster_declaration(roster: roster), ": List<", roster.row_type, "> = [\n"),
    entries,
    "]\n"
  )
}

// The ASCII case fold of a name or path, the key two names collide under on a case-insensitive
// filesystem. Only ASCII letters fold: a non-ASCII byte string compares as itself, so two names
// that differ only in a non-ASCII case are not claimed to collide.
fn ascii_case_fold_from(s: String, at: Int, acc: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  if at >= string_length(s: s) {
    acc
  } else {
    let ch = substring(s: s, start: at, end: at + 1)
    ascii_case_fold_from(
      s: s,
      at: at + 1,
      acc: list_snoc_item(xs: acc, item: ascii_lowercase(s: ch))
    )
  }
}

fn ascii_case_fold(s: String) -> String {
  join(ascii_case_fold_from(s: s, at: 0, acc: []), "")
}

// ---- lookup by name -------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A QUALIFIED REFERENCE IS SPLIT INTO MODULE AND MEMBER BY PROBING PREFIXES, LONGEST FIRST, and each
// probe is one exact read whose answer is tri-state: the file is present, the file is absent, or
// the read was refused (unreadable, the wrong kind, a transport failure). Only absence falls back to
// a shorter prefix. A refusal is NOT absence: treating an unreadable file as absent would let the
// shorter prefix answer for a module that exists, which is the silent widening DESIGN section 5
// forbids. No candidate present is an unknown module, never a guess.
//
// The lookup is two steps a driver alternates, so the READ SET is exactly the probes it asked for:
// reference_next_read names the next file to read, reference_after_probe consumes the answer. The
// driver that reads through a bound filesystem handler is effectful; resolve_reference_over runs the
// same two steps over a supplied observation and is the control for the decision itself.
//
// The cross-tree verdict (v2.std.cross_tree.import_model cross_tree_import_admitted) is decided HERE,
// before the read, because a candidate's tree and namespace are known from its name alone. Admission requires
// both namespaces to have a row in the layout, and there is no direction: a crossing between two declared
// namespaces is admitted either way, and an importer whose namespace has no row is refused without the
// candidate being read. A cross-tree CANDIDATE whose namespace has no row is skipped, not refused:
// longest-first probing asks `v2.std.text.String` (namespace `v2.std.text`, a module, not a row)
// before `v2.std.text`, and no module is stored in a namespace with no row.
type ModuleFileProbe
  = ModuleFilePresent { file: ModuleFileRef, content: String }
  | ModuleFileAbsent { file: ModuleFileRef }
  | ModuleFileRefused { file: ModuleFileRef, cause: String }

type PrefixAccumulator {
  names: List<String>
  current: String
}

fn prefix_accumulate(acc: PrefixAccumulator, segment: String) -> PrefixAccumulator {
  let next = if acc.current == "" { segment } else { concat(acc.current, ".", segment) }
  PrefixAccumulator { names: list_snoc_item(xs: acc.names, item: next), current: next }
}

// The dotted prefixes of a reference, longest first: `a.b.c` -> a.b.c, a.b, a.
fn reference_prefixes(reference: String) -> List<String> {
  list_reverse(xs: fold_list(
    xs: split(s: reference, delimiter: "."),
    empty: PrefixAccumulator { names: [], current: "" },
    cons: fn(acc, segment) { prefix_accumulate(acc: acc, segment: segment) }
  ).names)
}

type ReferenceNextRead
  = ReferenceNextReadFile { module: String, file: ModuleFileRef, rest: List<String> }
  | ReferenceNextReadDenied { module: String, file: ModuleFileRef, reason: CrossTreeDenialReason }
  | ReferenceNextReadNone { reference: String }

fn storage_active_trees() -> SourceRootSet {
  [V2Tree, DagTree]
}

fn storage_cross_tree_verdict(layout: SourceStorageLayout, from_tree: SourceRootRef, from_module: String, module: String, file: ModuleFileRef) -> CrossTreeAdmissionVerdict {
  cross_tree_import_admitted(
    edge: CrossTreeImport {
      from_tree: from_tree,
      from_namespace: module_namespace(name: from_module),
      to_tree: file.tree,
      to_namespace: module_namespace(name: module)
    },
    roots: storage_active_trees(),
    declared: layout.namespace_rows
  )
}

// A cross-tree candidate in a namespace with no row names no stored module (see above).
fn storage_candidate_unstorable(layout: SourceStorageLayout, from_tree: SourceRootRef, module: String, file: ModuleFileRef) -> Bool {
  !source_root_ref_eq(a: from_tree, b: file.tree) && !namespace_declared(rows: layout.namespace_rows, namespace: module_namespace(name: module))
}

fn reference_next_read_derived(
  layout: SourceStorageLayout,
  from_tree: SourceRootRef,
  from_module: String,
  module: String,
  file: ModuleFileRef,
  rest: List<String>
) -> ReferenceNextRead {
  match storage_cross_tree_verdict(layout: layout, from_tree: from_tree, from_module: from_module, module: module, file: file) {
    CrossTreeAdmitted => ReferenceNextReadFile { module: module, file: file, rest: rest }
    CrossTreeDenied { reason: reason } => ReferenceNextReadDenied { module: module, file: file, reason: reason }
  }
}

// The next file to read: the first remaining candidate that has a file at all. A candidate with no
// file (a namespace the layout elides, such as `v2` alone) is skipped, because it names a directory.
fn reference_next_read(
  layout: SourceStorageLayout,
  from_tree: SourceRootRef,
  from_module: String,
  reference: String,
  candidates: List<String>
) -> ReferenceNextRead {
  match list_head(xs: candidates) {
    Absent => ReferenceNextReadNone { reference: reference }
    Present { value: candidate } =>
      match module_file(layout: layout, name: candidate) {
        ModuleFileDerived { file: file } =>
          if storage_candidate_unstorable(layout: layout, from_tree: from_tree, module: candidate, file: file) {
            reference_next_read(layout: layout, from_tree: from_tree, from_module: from_module, reference: reference, candidates: skip(xs: candidates, n: 1))
          } else {
            reference_next_read_derived(layout: layout, from_tree: from_tree, from_module: from_module, module: candidate, file: file, rest: skip(xs: candidates, n: 1))
          }
        ModuleNameUnderivable { name: _, cause: _ } =>
          reference_next_read(layout: layout, from_tree: from_tree, from_module: from_module, reference: reference, candidates: skip(xs: candidates, n: 1))
      }
  }
}

type ReferenceLookup
  = ReferenceResolved { module: String, member: String, file: ModuleFileRef, content: String }
  | ReferenceUnknownModule { reference: String }
  | ReferenceRefused { module: String, file: ModuleFileRef, cause: String }
  | ReferenceModuleLineMissing { module: String, file: ModuleFileRef }
  | ReferenceModuleLineDisagrees { module: String, declared: String, file: ModuleFileRef }
  | ReferenceModuleHeaderRefused { module: String, file: ModuleFileRef, cause: String }
  | ReferenceCrossTreeDenied { module: String, file: ModuleFileRef, reason: CrossTreeDenialReason }

type ReferenceAdvance
  = ReferenceDecided { lookup: ReferenceLookup }
  | ReferenceContinue { rest: List<String> }

// The member of a reference once its module is known: the segments after the module's own.
fn reference_member(reference: String, module: String) -> String {
  let consumed = segment_count(segments: split(s: module, delimiter: "."))
  join(skip(xs: split(s: reference, delimiter: "."), n: consumed), ".")
}

fn reference_present(reader: ModuleHeaderReader, reference: String, module: String, file: ModuleFileRef, content: String) -> ReferenceLookup {
  match module_header_read(reader: reader, file: module_file_key(file: file), content: content) {
    ModuleHeaderAbsent => ReferenceModuleLineMissing { module: module, file: file }
    ModuleHeaderRefused { cause: cause } => ReferenceModuleHeaderRefused { module: module, file: file, cause: cause }
    ModuleHeaderDeclared { module: declared } =>
      if declared == module {
        ReferenceResolved { module: module, member: reference_member(reference: reference, module: module), file: file, content: content }
      } else {
        ReferenceModuleLineDisagrees { module: module, declared: declared, file: file }
      }
  }
}

fn reference_after_probe(
  reader: ModuleHeaderReader,
  reference: String,
  module: String,
  rest: List<String>,
  probe: ModuleFileProbe
) -> ReferenceAdvance {
  match probe {
    ModuleFilePresent { file: file, content: content } =>
      ReferenceDecided { lookup: reference_present(reader: reader, reference: reference, module: module, file: file, content: content) }
    ModuleFileAbsent { file: _ } => ReferenceContinue { rest: rest }
    ModuleFileRefused { file: file, cause: cause } =>
      ReferenceDecided { lookup: ReferenceRefused { module: module, file: file, cause: cause } }
  }
}

fn module_file_ref_eq(a: ModuleFileRef, b: ModuleFileRef) -> Bool {
  source_root_ref_label(tree: a.tree) == source_root_ref_label(tree: b.tree) && a.relative_path == b.relative_path
}

fn module_file_probe_file(probe: ModuleFileProbe) -> ModuleFileRef {
  match probe {
    ModuleFilePresent { file: file, content: _ } => file
    ModuleFileAbsent { file: file } => file
    ModuleFileRefused { file: file, cause: _ } => file
  }
}

// What a supplied observation says about one file. A file the observation does not mention is
// absent, which is what a storage binding answers for a path that is not there.
fn observed_probe_of(observed: List<ModuleFileProbe>, file: ModuleFileRef) -> ModuleFileProbe {
  match list_find_first(xs: observed, predicate: fn(probe) { module_file_ref_eq(a: module_file_probe_file(probe: probe), b: file) }) {
    Present { value: probe } => probe
    Absent => ModuleFileAbsent { file: file }
  }
}

fn resolve_reference_from(
  reader: ModuleHeaderReader,
  layout: SourceStorageLayout,
  from_tree: SourceRootRef,
  from_module: String,
  reference: String,
  candidates: List<String>,
  observed: List<ModuleFileProbe>
) -> ReferenceLookup {
  match reference_next_read(layout: layout, from_tree: from_tree, from_module: from_module, reference: reference, candidates: candidates) {
    ReferenceNextReadNone { reference: unknown } => ReferenceUnknownModule { reference: unknown }
    ReferenceNextReadDenied { module: module, file: file, reason: reason } =>
      ReferenceCrossTreeDenied { module: module, file: file, reason: reason }
    ReferenceNextReadFile { module: module, file: file, rest: rest } =>
      match reference_after_probe(reader: reader, reference: reference, module: module, rest: rest, probe: observed_probe_of(observed: observed, file: file)) {
        ReferenceDecided { lookup: lookup } => lookup
        ReferenceContinue { rest: remaining } =>
          resolve_reference_from(reader: reader, layout: layout, from_tree: from_tree, from_module: from_module, reference: reference, candidates: remaining, observed: observed)
      }
  }
}

// The lookup run over a supplied observation: the decision procedure, without the reads.
fn resolve_reference_over(
  reader: ModuleHeaderReader,
  layout: SourceStorageLayout,
  from_tree: SourceRootRef,
  from_module: String,
  reference: String,
  observed: List<ModuleFileProbe>
) -> ReferenceLookup {
  resolve_reference_from(
    reader: reader,
    layout: layout,
    from_tree: from_tree,
    from_module: from_module,
    reference: reference,
    candidates: reference_prefixes(reference: reference),
    observed: observed
  )
}

// ---- the layout check -----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// ONE MODE: HOLD OR REFUSE. The check is a pure fold over three supplied values -- the storage
// layout, the namespace rows the repository declares, and the header (location and `module` line)
// of every governed `.dag` file. The population is enumerated by the native CLI through the bound
// filesystem handler; nothing here reads, and the check reads headers, never bodies. Every refusal is typed and located, and the verdict carries all
// of them: a line stopped is analyzed whole.
//
// A namespace row is a namespace that DIRECTLY contains modules; its ancestors are derived and carry
// no row. The rows are checked as an identity join in both directions -- a file in a namespace with
// no row is UndeclaredNamespace, a row whose namespace holds no module is NamespaceRowWithoutModules
// -- never a count, so a swap of one namespace for another cannot pass.
//
// Whether a file is generated is NOT a fact of the layout: `gunbc.generated_artifact_registry` is that
// fact's single authority, and this check neither reads nor restates it -- a generated module is held
// to its rule position like any other. The registry's converse obligation (a registered `.dag`
// artifact's producer declares the module its registered location is the rule position of) is
// owned beside the registry, not here, so the compiler's own CLI closure does not reach the fleet
// hosts and plans the registry enumerates.
type LayoutFileHeader {
  file: ModuleFileRef
  declared: ModuleHeaderRead
}

type LayoutRefusal
  = UndeclaredNamespace { file: ModuleFileRef, namespace: String }
  | NamespaceRowWithoutModules { namespace: String }
  | NotAtRulePosition { file: ModuleFileRef, declared: String, expected: ModuleFileRef }
  | DeclaredModuleUnderivable { file: ModuleFileRef, declared: String, cause: String }
  | FileNotNamed { file: ModuleFileRef, cause: String }
  | ModuleLineMissing { file: ModuleFileRef }
  | ModuleHeaderUnreadable { file: ModuleFileRef, cause: String }
  | DuplicateModule { module: String, files: List<ModuleFileRef> }

type LayoutVerdict
  = LayoutHolds
  | LayoutRefused { refusals: List<LayoutRefusal> }

// The namespace a module name lives in: the name without its last segment.
fn module_namespace(name: String) -> String {
  let segments = split(s: name, delimiter: ".")
  join(take_segments(segments: segments, n: segment_count(segments: segments) - 1), ".")
}

fn take_segments_from(segments: List<String>, n: Int, acc: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  if n <= 0 {
    acc
  } else {
    match list_head(xs: segments) {
      Absent => acc
      Present { value: segment } =>
        take_segments_from(segments: skip(xs: segments, n: 1), n: n - 1, acc: list_snoc_item(xs: acc, item: segment))
    }
  }
}

fn take_segments(segments: List<String>, n: Int) -> List<String> {
  take_segments_from(segments: segments, n: n, acc: [])
}

fn module_file_key(file: ModuleFileRef) -> String {
  concat(source_root_ref_label(tree: file.tree), ":", file.relative_path)
}

fn text_set_of(names: List<String>) -> Map<String, Bool> {
  fold_list(xs: names, empty: empty_map(), cons: fn(index, name) { map_insert(m: index, key: name, value: true) })
}

fn text_set_has(set: Map<String, Bool>, name: String) -> Bool {
  match map_lookup(m: set, key: name) {
    Present { value: _ } => true
    Absent => false
  }
}

// A header's refusals other than the duplicate and registry joins, which look across headers.
fn header_position_refusals(layout: SourceStorageLayout, namespaces: Map<String, Bool>, header: LayoutFileHeader) -> List<LayoutRefusal> {
  match module_name_for_file(layout: layout, file: header.file) {
    ModuleFileNotNamed { file: file, cause: cause } => [FileNotNamed { file: file, cause: cause }]
    ModuleNameDerived { name: positional } =>
      concat(
        header_namespace_refusals(namespaces: namespaces, header: header, positional: positional),
        header_declaration_refusals(layout: layout, header: header, positional: positional)
      )
  }
}

fn header_namespace_refusals(namespaces: Map<String, Bool>, header: LayoutFileHeader, positional: String) -> List<LayoutRefusal> {
  let namespace = module_namespace(name: positional)
  if text_set_has(set: namespaces, name: namespace) {
    []
  } else {
    [UndeclaredNamespace { file: header.file, namespace: namespace }]
  }
}

fn header_declaration_refusals(layout: SourceStorageLayout, header: LayoutFileHeader, positional: String) -> List<LayoutRefusal> {
  match header.declared {
    ModuleHeaderAbsent => [ModuleLineMissing { file: header.file }]
    ModuleHeaderRefused { cause: cause } => [ModuleHeaderUnreadable { file: header.file, cause: cause }]
    ModuleHeaderDeclared { module: declared } =>
      match module_file(layout: layout, name: declared) {
        ModuleNameUnderivable { name: _, cause: cause } =>
          [DeclaredModuleUnderivable { file: header.file, declared: declared, cause: cause }]
        ModuleFileDerived { file: expected } =>
          if module_file_ref_eq(a: expected, b: header.file) {
            []
          } else {
            [NotAtRulePosition { file: header.file, declared: declared, expected: expected }]
          }
      }
  }
}

fn no_refusals() -> List<LayoutRefusal> {
  []
}

// Folded from the last header to the first, each header's few refusals joined ahead of the rest, so the
// refusals come out in the headers' order at the cost of the population and not its square.
fn headers_position_refusals(layout: SourceStorageLayout, namespaces: Map<String, Bool>, headers: List<LayoutFileHeader>) -> List<LayoutRefusal> {
  fold_list(
    xs: list_reverse(xs: headers),
    empty: no_refusals(),
    cons: fn(refusals, header) { concat(header_position_refusals(layout: layout, namespaces: namespaces, header: header), refusals) }
  )
}

// The headers grouped by the ASCII case fold of the module they declare: two files declaring one
// module, or modules differing only by case (which collide on a case-insensitive filesystem), land
// in one group. Both lists are built by prepending, so the grouping costs the size of the population
// and not its square; `order` and each group's members therefore run newest first, and the readers
// restore the order the headers came in.
type DeclaredGroups {
  order: List<String>
  members: Map<String, List<LayoutFileHeader>>
}

fn declared_group_add(groups: DeclaredGroups, header: LayoutFileHeader) -> DeclaredGroups {
  match header.declared {
    ModuleHeaderAbsent => groups
    ModuleHeaderRefused { cause: _ } => groups
    ModuleHeaderDeclared { module: declared } => declared_group_insert(groups: groups, key: ascii_case_fold(s: declared), header: header)
  }
}

fn declared_group_insert(groups: DeclaredGroups, key: String, header: LayoutFileHeader) -> DeclaredGroups {
  match map_lookup(m: groups.members, key: key) {
    Absent =>
      DeclaredGroups {
        order: Cons { head: key, tail: groups.order },
        members: map_insert(m: groups.members, key: key, value: [header])
      }
    Present { value: seen } =>
      DeclaredGroups {
        order: groups.order,
        members: map_insert(m: groups.members, key: key, value: Cons { head: header, tail: seen })
      }
  }
}

fn duplicate_refusal_of(groups: DeclaredGroups, key: String) -> List<LayoutRefusal> {
  match map_lookup(m: groups.members, key: key) {
    Absent => []
    Present { value: members } =>
      if length(xs: members) < 2 {
        []
      } else {
        [DuplicateModule { module: key, files: list_map(xs: list_reverse(xs: members), f: fn(header) { header.file }) }]
      }
  }
}

fn headers_duplicate_refusals(headers: List<LayoutFileHeader>) -> List<LayoutRefusal> {
  let groups = fold_list(
    xs: headers,
    empty: DeclaredGroups { order: [], members: empty_map() },
    cons: fn(acc, header) { declared_group_add(groups: acc, header: header) }
  )
  fold_list(
    xs: groups.order,
    empty: no_refusals(),
    cons: fn(refusals, key) { concat(duplicate_refusal_of(groups: groups, key: key), refusals) }
  )
}

// The namespaces the observed files actually occupy, by position.
fn occupied_namespaces(layout: SourceStorageLayout, headers: List<LayoutFileHeader>) -> Map<String, Bool> {
  fold_list(
    xs: headers,
    empty: empty_map(),
    cons: fn(occupied, header) {
      match module_name_for_file(layout: layout, file: header.file) {
        ModuleNameDerived { name: positional } => map_insert(m: occupied, key: module_namespace(name: positional), value: true)
        ModuleFileNotNamed { file: _, cause: _ } => occupied
      }
    }
  )
}

// One refusal per declared row no file occupies, in the rows' order.
fn namespace_row_refusals(occupied: Map<String, Bool>, namespaces: List<String>) -> List<LayoutRefusal> {
  list_map(
    xs: filter(xs: namespaces, predicate: fn(namespace) { text_set_has(set: occupied, name: namespace) == false }),
    f: fn(namespace) { NamespaceRowWithoutModules { namespace: namespace } }
  )
}

fn layout_refusals(
  layout: SourceStorageLayout,
  namespaces: List<String>,
  headers: List<LayoutFileHeader>
) -> List<LayoutRefusal> {
  let declared = text_set_of(names: namespaces)
  concat(
    concat(
      headers_position_refusals(layout: layout, namespaces: declared, headers: headers),
      headers_duplicate_refusals(headers: headers)
    ),
    namespace_row_refusals(occupied: occupied_namespaces(layout: layout, headers: headers), namespaces: namespaces)
  )
}

fn layout_check(
  layout: SourceStorageLayout,
  namespaces: List<String>,
  headers: List<LayoutFileHeader>
) -> LayoutVerdict {
  let refusals = layout_refusals(layout: layout, namespaces: namespaces, headers: headers)
  if is_empty(xs: refusals) {
    LayoutHolds {}
  } else {
    LayoutRefused { refusals: refusals }
  }
}

// ---- rendering the verdict ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// One line per refusal and none capped: the verdict carries all of them so a line stopped is analyzed
// whole, and each line names the file by its tree and path (module_file_key) so the author can act on
// it where it is.
fn layout_refusal_text(refusal: LayoutRefusal) -> String {
  match refusal {
    UndeclaredNamespace { file: file, namespace: namespace } =>
      concat(module_file_key(file: file), ": namespace ", namespace, " has no declared row")
    NamespaceRowWithoutModules { namespace: namespace } =>
      concat("namespace ", namespace, " is declared but no governed file holds a module in it")
    NotAtRulePosition { file: file, declared: declared, expected: expected } =>
      concat(module_file_key(file: file), ": declares module ", declared, ", whose rule position is ", module_file_key(file: expected))
    DeclaredModuleUnderivable { file: file, declared: declared, cause: cause } =>
      concat(module_file_key(file: file), ": declares module ", declared, ", which names no storable position: ", cause)
    FileNotNamed { file: file, cause: cause } =>
      concat(module_file_key(file: file), ": the file's position names no module: ", cause)
    ModuleLineMissing { file: file } => concat(module_file_key(file: file), ": no module line")
    ModuleHeaderUnreadable { file: file, cause: cause } => concat(module_file_key(file: file), ": the module header does not parse: ", cause)
    DuplicateModule { module: module, files: files } =>
      concat("module ", module, " is declared by ", join(list_map(xs: files, f: fn(file) { module_file_key(file: file) }), ", "))
  }
}

fn layout_verdict_text(verdict: LayoutVerdict) -> String {
  match verdict {
    LayoutHolds => "layout holds: every governed file is at the rule position of the module it declares"
    LayoutRefused { refusals: refusals } => join(list_map(xs: refusals, f: fn(refusal) { layout_refusal_text(refusal: refusal) }), "\n")
  }
}

fn layout_verdict_holds(verdict: LayoutVerdict) -> Bool {
  match verdict {
    LayoutHolds => true
    LayoutRefused { refusals: _ } => false
  }
}
